# Git stores whatever bytes a tool wrote unless this file says otherwise. `core.autocrlf` is `false`
# here, so git performs no conversion at all and an editor that emits CRLF permanently flips the
# stored file. Measured 2026-08-05 across four repos: a 945-line change read as 6097 lines, which
# would have destroyed `git blame` on five files. The real edit was invisible inside the noise.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise to LF in the repository on commit while leaving working copies
# alone. It is incremental — only files that are committed get normalised — so it neither rewrites
# history nor requires a `git add --renormalize`.
* text=auto

# Line endings that are load-bearing, not cosmetic. A shell script or a Dockerfile with CRLF fails
# inside a Linux container with an error that names the interpreter, not the line endings.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Windows-native files keep CRLF deliberately.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
